{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1775738902,"nonce":"W16cDN7OH9NDLtMI4WdeGuHAS7Q=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM78RCBT","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/45","origin":"github"},"title":"Investigate RUTX11 as GPS-synced NTP server for BizzyBoat","message":"## Background\n\nBizzyBoat needs a reliable time source for its onboard network. The Teltonika RUTX11 router has a built-in GPS receiver and could potentially serve NTP to LAN clients. Initial testing showed that the router serves incorrect time after a power cycle (no battery-backed RTC) until GPS acquires a fix.\n\n## Goal\n\nInvestigate the feasibility of configuring the RUTX11 as an NTP server for the onboard network, using:\n- Internet NTP sources (pool.ntp.org, etc.) for immediate sync at boot\n- GPS time as the preferred source once a fix is acquired\n\n## Investigation Areas\n\n- Current NTP daemon on RUTX11 (chrony vs ntpd)\n- GPS time integration options\n- Whether internet NTP fallback can prevent wrong-time-at-boot problem\n- Overall feasibility and accuracy for ROS 2 operations\n\n## Deliverable\n\nInvestigation log documenting findings and configuration recommendations.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `claude-opus-4-6`","files":null}]}